WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.630
An alkene adds bromine across its double
bond in seconds, even in the dark.

2
00:00:05.680 --> 00:00:08.110
Benzene is often drawn
with three double bonds,

3
00:00:08.160 --> 00:00:10.570
so you might expect it to do the same.

4
00:00:10.620 --> 00:00:15.110
Instead, benzene reacts with bromine
only when a catalyst is added.

5
00:00:15.160 --> 00:00:19.290
And then a bromine atom takes
the place of one hydrogen atom.

6
00:00:19.340 --> 00:00:22.770
Why does a hydrogen get swapped,
rather than bromine adding?

7
00:00:22.820 --> 00:00:27.190
In this mission, you'll find out why
benzene's ring attracts electrophiles,

8
00:00:27.240 --> 00:00:29.170
just as an alkene's double bond does.

9
00:00:29.220 --> 00:00:33.090
You'll draw the mechanism of
an electrophilic substitution,

10
00:00:33.140 --> 00:00:37.110
curly arrow by curly arrow,
using the nitration of benzene.

11
00:00:37.160 --> 00:00:42.270
Your mission: explain why benzene swaps
a hydrogen instead of adding bromine.
